          aria-expanded:text-text-100 h-8 rounded-md px-3 min-w-[4rem] active:scale-[0.985] whitespace-nowrap !text-xs pl-2.5 pr-2 gap-1 !font-base select-none !pl-2 !pr-1" type="button" id="radix-_r_ov_" aria-haspopup="menu" aria-expanded="false" data-state="closed">Reintentar<div class="flex items-center justify-center text-text-500" style="width: 20px; height: 20px;"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="shrink-0 text-text-500" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M14.128 7.16482C14.3126 6.95983 14.6298 6.94336 14.835 7.12771C15.0402 7.31242 15.0567 7.62952 14.8721 7.83477L10.372 12.835L10.2939 12.9053C10.2093 12.9667 10.1063 13 9.99995 13C9.85833 12.9999 9.72264 12.9402 9.62788 12.835L5.12778 7.83477L5.0682 7.75273C4.95072 7.55225 4.98544 7.28926 5.16489 7.12771C5.34445 6.96617 5.60969 6.95939 5.79674 7.09744L5.87193 7.16482L9.99995 11.7519L14.128 7.16482Z"></path></svg></div></button></div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div data-test-render-count="4"><div class="mb-1 mt-1"><div class="group  relative  inline-flex  gap-2  bg-bg-300  rounded-xl  pl-2.5  py-2.5  break-words  text-text-100  transition-all  max-w-[75ch]  flex-col pr-6" style="opacity: 1; transform: none;"><div class="flex flex-row gap-2"><div class="shrink-0"><div class="flex shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full font-bold select-none h-7 w-7 text-[12px] bg-text-200 text-bg-100">NG</div></div><div data-testid="user-message" class="font-ui grid grid-cols-1 gap-2 py-0.5 text-[0.9375rem] leading-6 tracking-tight"><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Here’s a draft of a comparative commentary you could use as the basis for a short note or even a publishable comment. I’ve written it in an academic style but kept it explanatory enough to make the overlaps clear.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">---</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Commentary: Effective Fluid Backreaction and the Relativistic Scalar Vector Plenum</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Introduction</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Giani, von Marttens, and Camilleri (2025) have recently introduced a two-fluid model that treats non-virialized collapsing structures and expanding voids as effective cosmological fluids. This approach provides a phenomenological framework to capture backreaction effects of the cosmic web on the large-scale evolution of the Universe, with the potential to alleviate the Hubble and  tensions without invoking new fundamental physics.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">In parallel, the Relativistic Scalar Vector Plenum (RSVP) framework develops a field-theoretic description of cosmology in which scalar density (), vector flow (), and entropy () fields evolve non-linearly, with apparent cosmic acceleration emerging from entropic smoothing rather than spacetime expansion. The close correspondence between the two approaches is striking: the effective fluids of Giani et al. are precisely the coarse-grained manifestations of RSVP’s scalar–vector–entropy dynamics.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">---</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Effective Fluids in Cosmology</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">In the Giani–von Marttens–Camilleri model:</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Collapsing overdense regions are encoded as a fluid  with equation of state .</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Expanding voids are encoded as a fluid  with equation of state .</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">These components exchange energy with pressureless dust () through couplings .</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">The total effective energy density  is conserved, but the effective global equation of state</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">w_{\rm tot}=\frac{\rho_c w_c+\rho_v w<em>v}{\rho</em>{\rm tot}}</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">This encoding allows cosmic voids and collapsing clusters to be treated as if they were dark-energy-like fluids, reconciling observations without exotic new particles or fields.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">---</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">RSVP Field-Theoretic Encoding</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">RSVP posits that:</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Collapse modes correspond to regions where , leading to negentropic compression and effective negative divergence.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Void modes correspond to regions where , driving entropic expansion.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Lamphron–lamphrodyne transfers describe the exchange of energy between dust-like density, collapsing modes, and expanding modes—mathematically identical to  and .</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">The apparent acceleration is not fundamental dark energy but arises from entropic redistribution in the plenum.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Thus, where the effective-fluid model introduces new phenomenological components, RSVP derives them as emergent behaviors of the scalar–vector–entropy fields.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">---</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Correspondence Between Frameworks</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Giani–von Marttens–Camilleri    RSVP Analogue</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">: collapsing fluid    Negentropic compressive field regions ()
: void fluid    Entropic expansive field regions ()
Couplings     Lamphron–lamphrodyne transfer terms
Effective EoS     RSVP’s global entropy-weighted pressure-to-density ratio
Effective-fluid backreaction    RSVP’s coarse-grained scalar–vector–entropy dynamics</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">---</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Implications</p><ol class="[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-7"><li class="whitespace-normal break-words">Phenomenology vs. Microphysics: The effective-fluid model is a phenomenological “bookkeeping” scheme; RSVP provides a microphysical field origin for the same terms.</li></ol><ol class="[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-7"><li class="whitespace-normal break-words">Cosmological Tensions: Both approaches can alleviate the Hubble and  tensions, but RSVP predicts further deviations in redshift–distance and entropy production that could be tested observationally.</li></ol><ol class="[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-7"><li class="whitespace-normal break-words">Scaling Ratchets: Just as computation ratcheted into ASCII and cinema into 2D frames, cosmology ratcheted into ΛCDM perturbation theory. Effective fluids reopen suppressed encodings of non-linear structure; RSVP goes further, embedding them in a universal field theory.</li></ol><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">---</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Conclusion</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">The new effective-fluid model of collapsing and expanding regions represents a significant shift in mainstream cosmology, acknowledging that non-linear backreaction can affect the large-scale evolution of the Universe. From the RSVP perspective, this development is especially important: it demonstrates that what RSVP treats as fundamental scalar–vector–entropy dynamics can be recognized in phenomenological fluid form within ΛCDM extensions.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">In this sense, the effective-fluid approach and RSVP are not competitors but different levels of the same description: the former a practical parameterization, the latter a candidate for the underlying field theory. Future work should investigate how RSVP predictions for observables (luminosity distance, BAO, ) map directly onto the effective-fluid framework, enabling empirical tests of whether entropic backreaction is sufficient to replace dark energy altogether.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">---</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Would you like me to tighten this into a letter-to-the-editor style format (short, 1–2 pages, with references), so it could be submitted directly as a response to Physical Review Letters or Phys. Rev. D? That would make it look less like an essay and more like a formal scientific commentary.</p></div></div><div class="absolute bottom-0 right-2 pointer-events-none" style="transform: none;"><div class="rounded-lg transition min-w-max pointer-events-auto translate-y-4 bg-bg-100/80 backdrop-blur-sm translate-x-1 group-hover:translate-x-0.5 border-0.5 border-border-300 p-0.5 shadow-sm opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100"><div class="text-text-300 flex items-stretch justify-between"><button class="inline-flex
