<p class="whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Scale vs. expansion distinction</strong>: While Neil Turok and others have explored conformal cyclic models, the observational evidence strongly indicates genuine spatial expansion, not just scale changes. The key evidence includes:</p>
<ul class="[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words">Surface brightness dimming of galaxies follows (1+z)⁴ rather than (1+z)²</li>
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words">Time dilation in distant supernovae and other transient phenomena</li>
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words">Angular diameter-redshift relationships match expansion predictions</li>
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words">Nucleosynthesis calculations require specific thermal history of genuine expansion</li>
</ul>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>"Smoothing" interpretation problems</strong>: Your description of expansion as "smoothing out" contradicts observed structure formation. The universe is becoming <em>more</em> structured over time, not smoother. Galaxy clusters, filaments, and voids grow more pronounced as time progresses, opposite to your smoothing scenario.</p>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>CMB formation timing</strong>: The claim that "hydrogen atoms formed everywhere in the universe at once" misunderstands recombination. It occurred gradually over ~100,000 years as the universe cooled through specific temperature thresholds. The CMB photons we observe were last scattered at different times and places as the recombination front propagated.</p>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Energy conservation issues</strong>: Your statement that there's "no where to move energy into except neighbouring regions" reflects a misunderstanding of general relativity. In expanding spacetime, energy can be created or destroyed - photons lose energy to the expansion itself, not to neighboring matter.</p>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Poincaré recurrence</strong>: Applying Poincaré recurrence time to cosmology assumes the universe is a closed finite system, which contradicts observational evidence for spatial flatness and likely infinite extent.</p>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words">The theoretical framework you're developing needs to address these specific observational constraints quantitatively, not just provide alternative conceptual descriptions. The standard model's strength lies in its precise numerical predictions across multiple independent observations.</p></div></div><div class="flex flex-col gap-2 py-2"></div></div><div class="absolute bottom-0 right-2 pointer-events-none" style="transform: none;"><div class="rounded-lg transition min-w-max pointer-events-auto translate-x-2 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100"><div class="text-text-300 flex items-stretch justify-between"><button class="inline-flex
