<p class="whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Inflation energy storage misconception</strong>: The inflaton field didn't "lend" energy that particles are now "paying back." Inflation stretched space exponentially, diluting matter and radiation densities to nearly zero. The gravitational potential energy in today's structures comes from matter falling into potential wells <em>after</em> inflation ended, not from paying back inflationary energy debt.</p>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Energy source confusion</strong>: The energy driving structure formation comes from the kinetic energy of matter as it falls into gravitational wells - this is converted gravitational potential energy from the initial density fluctuations, not stored inflationary energy. The inflaton field decayed into standard model particles at the end of inflation (reheating), and that energy became the thermal bath of the early universe.</p>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Gravitational potential energy origins</strong>: Gravitational potential energy exists whenever matter is distributed non-uniformly. The negative potential energy of bound structures (galaxies, clusters) is balanced by the positive kinetic energy of expansion and random motions. This energy balance doesn't depend on inflation having "lent" anything.</p>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Observational timing problems</strong>: Your mechanism would predict that gravitational potential energy release should correlate with redshift in specific ways that differ from expansion predictions. The fact that distant supernovae appear dimmer than expected in a decelerating universe actually argues <em>against</em> additional energy sources from gravitational collapse.</p>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words">The mathematical framework of general relativity already accounts for how gravitational potential energy and matter energy evolve during cosmic expansion. Your model needs to specify how entropy dynamics produces identical observational consequences through completely different physics - a much more demanding theoretical challenge than energy bookkeeping.</p></div></div><div class="flex flex-col gap-2 py-2"></div></div><div class="absolute bottom-0 right-2 pointer-events-none" style="transform: none;"><div class="rounded-lg transition min-w-max pointer-events-auto translate-x-2 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100"><div class="text-text-300 flex items-stretch justify-between"><button class="inline-flex
