<p class="whitespace-normal break-words">Your claim that "the inflaton field does explain gravitational potential - it pushed particles apart but they are falling back together releasing the energy borrowed from inflation" contradicts this established physics in several ways:</p>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Energy accounting error</strong>: The lecture clearly explains that gravitational potential energy is negative (becomes more negative as objects fall together), while kinetic energy is positive. The total energy can be positive, negative, or zero, but there's no "borrowing" from inflation. The inflaton field decayed into radiation and matter at reheating - it didn't create an energy debt.</p>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Misunderstanding potential energy</strong>: When the lecturer says gravitational energy is "minus GMm/r," this means it becomes more negative (lower total energy) as objects come closer together. This isn't "releasing borrowed energy" - it's converting gravitational potential energy into kinetic energy, exactly as described.</p>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Inflation's actual role</strong>: Inflation created the initial density fluctuations that later grew into structure, but it didn't store energy that's being "paid back." The lecture explains that whatever launched the Big Bang provided enormous initial kinetic energy, which has been decreasing ever since due to gravitational attraction.</p>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Critical density relationship</strong>: The lecture shows that whether the universe expands forever or recollapses depends entirely on whether its density exceeds the critical density. This relationship comes from the balance of kinetic and gravitational energy, not from inflation energy storage.</p>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words">Your model would need to explain why observations match these standard energy relationships if the underlying physics were fundamentally different. The mathematics of the Friedmann equation constrains how energy components can evolve - you can't simply reinterpret the energy sources without changing the predicted observational consequences.</p></div></div><div class="flex flex-col gap-2 py-2"></div></div><div class="absolute bottom-0 right-2 pointer-events-none" style="transform: none;"><div class="rounded-lg transition min-w-max pointer-events-auto translate-x-2 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100"><div class="text-text-300 flex items-stretch justify-between"><button class="inline-flex
