<p class="whitespace-normal break-words">However, the correspondence has significant limitations:</p>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Scale mismatch</strong>: Burton works at Planck scale (10^-35 m) with quantum gravity effects, while your cosmological applications operate at Mpc scales (10^22 m). The connection across 57 orders of magnitude in scale requires justification.</p>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Different entropy sources</strong>: Burton's entropy comes from quantum curvature fluctuations in spacetime geometry itself. Your RSVP entropy field S appears to be a macroscopic thermodynamic quantity. These are conceptually and mathematically distinct entropy types.</p>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Cosmological constant interpretation</strong>: Burton derives a small positive cosmological constant from entropy maximization, supporting standard ΛCDM. Your framework rejects the cosmological constant as an "encoding artifact." These are contradictory positions.</p>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Mathematical formalism differences</strong>: Burton works within general relativity, deriving Einstein's field equations as Ehrenfest equations from quantum statistical mechanics. RSVP appears to replace general relativity with scalar-vector-entropy field dynamics. The mathematical structures are incompatible.</p>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Empirical status</strong>: Burton's work operates within established quantum field theory and general relativity frameworks. RSVP proposes replacing expansion cosmology entirely. The theoretical conservatism differs dramatically.</p>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words">While both approaches emphasize entropy's fundamental role, they address different scales, use different mathematical machinery, and make contradictory claims about cosmological expansion. The correspondence appears more analogical than mathematical. A rigorous comparison would need to specify exactly how Burton's Planck-scale entropy dynamics produce your macroscopic field equations - a derivation that doesn't appear in either framework currently.</p></div></div><div class="flex flex-col gap-2 py-2"></div></div><div class="absolute bottom-0 right-2 pointer-events-none" style="transform: none;"><div class="rounded-lg transition min-w-max pointer-events-auto translate-x-2 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100"><div class="text-text-300 flex items-stretch justify-between"><button class="inline-flex
