<p class="whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Theoretical scope differences</strong>: Burton works at the Planck scale with quantum gravity, deriving Einstein's field equations as Ehrenfest equations from entropy maximization of curvature fluctuations. Your RSVP operates at cosmological scales with macroscopic scalar-vector-entropy fields. These are fundamentally different physical regimes.</p>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Entropy interpretation</strong>: Burton's entropy comes from quantum statistical mechanics of spacetime geometry itself - it's counting microstates of the Riemann tensor. Your RSVP entropy field S appears to be a classical thermodynamic variable. These represent different physical quantities despite using the same symbol.</p>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Cosmological predictions</strong>: Burton derives a small positive cosmological constant from his entropy maximization, supporting standard ΛCDM expansion. RSVP rejects the cosmological constant and expansion itself. These frameworks make contradictory cosmological predictions.</p>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Mathematical structure</strong>: Burton works within differential geometry and general relativity, showing how quantum fluctuations produce classical field equations. RSVP proposes replacing general relativity with alternative field dynamics. The mathematical foundations are incompatible.</p>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Scale bridging problem</strong>: Creating a "Rosetta stone" between these frameworks requires demonstrating how Planck-scale quantum curvature entropy connects to cosmological-scale RSVP dynamics. This bridge across ~60 orders of magnitude in scale and energy is not established in either work.</p>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words">While both frameworks emphasize entropy's fundamental role, they operate in different physical regimes with contradictory cosmological implications. The correspondence appears conceptual rather than mathematical. A rigorous comparison would need to show explicit derivations connecting Burton's quantum statistical mechanics to RSVP's classical field theory - work that hasn't been done in either framework.</p></div></div><div class="flex flex-col gap-2 py-2"></div></div><div class="absolute bottom-0 right-2 pointer-events-none" style="transform: none;"><div class="rounded-lg transition min-w-max pointer-events-auto translate-x-2 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100"><div class="text-text-300 flex items-stretch justify-between"><button class="inline-flex
